WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.501 --> 00:00:02.755
- Today we're gonna talk
about the dos and don'ts

00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:05.026
of your first week in law school.

00:00:05.026 --> 00:00:07.254
And I know you're
freaking out, that's okay,

00:00:07.254 --> 00:00:09.282
don't worry about it because none of this

00:00:09.282 --> 00:00:11.340
is going to be on the test.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.923
(short jingle)

00:00:16.424 --> 00:00:18.170
The first week of law school can seem

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.084
completely overwhelming.

00:00:20.084 --> 00:00:21.887
Just take a step back and realize

00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:24.076
that it's all going to be okay.

00:00:24.076 --> 00:00:26.028
Everyone feels overwhelmed.

00:00:26.028 --> 00:00:27.619
You're thrown into a situation

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:29.170
that you've never been in before.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.753
You're learning about subjects
that you didn't know existed.

00:00:31.753 --> 00:00:33.685
It's probably going over your head.

00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:35.175
You probably feel stressed out,

00:00:35.175 --> 00:00:36.434
you might be a little freaked out,

00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:38.511
that's okay, everyone feels that way.

00:00:38.511 --> 00:00:40.778
But let's talk about
all the dos and don'ts

00:00:40.778 --> 00:00:42.705
of your first week of law school.

00:00:42.705 --> 00:00:44.839
Everything you can do
to maximize your chances

00:00:44.839 --> 00:00:47.135
of success later on and
everything you can do

00:00:47.135 --> 00:00:49.464
to minimize your stress in the present.

00:00:49.464 --> 00:00:52.511
You should understand that
the way that law school

00:00:52.511 --> 00:00:54.916
is taught is completely backwards.

00:00:54.916 --> 00:00:58.629
You learn the most antiquated arcane cases

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:02.296
early on in the semester and
there is very little chance

00:01:02.296 --> 00:01:05.804
that any of those cases will
be used on the final exam.

00:01:05.804 --> 00:01:08.241
For example, if you are in Property

00:01:08.241 --> 00:01:10.830
you might learn about Pierson versus Post

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.580
which is the case where
two men pursued a fox

00:01:15.496 --> 00:01:17.457
but one man killed it and carried it away,

00:01:17.457 --> 00:01:20.216
and it's a dispute about who
had possession of the fox.

00:01:20.216 --> 00:01:24.481
And the rule of law is that
mere pursuit of the animal

00:01:24.481 --> 00:01:27.478
itself does not give you a right to the

00:01:27.478 --> 00:01:29.336
ownership of the animal.

00:01:29.336 --> 00:01:33.406
But guess what, there are
almost no exams in the history

00:01:33.406 --> 00:01:36.662
of Property where citing
to Pierson versus Post

00:01:36.662 --> 00:01:39.574
is a way to get points on the final exam.

00:01:39.574 --> 00:01:42.500
It's weird because, strangely enough,

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.048
pursuing animals is not
something that is often done

00:01:46.048 --> 00:01:48.577
in modern society, and
therefore those situations

00:01:48.577 --> 00:01:50.292
just don't come up that much.

00:01:50.292 --> 00:01:53.734
So don't worry that every
single class seems to start

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:56.439
with these antiquated
cases that will not be

00:01:56.439 --> 00:01:59.869
on your final exam and really
don't do much to further

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:02.448
your understanding of the substantive law.

00:02:02.448 --> 00:02:05.917
So don't feel bad if you
feel completely overwhelmed

00:02:05.917 --> 00:02:10.541
with the arcane law from the
Court of Exchequer in England,

00:02:10.541 --> 00:02:14.109
it's a way to ease into
the semester and it's not

00:02:14.109 --> 00:02:15.389
information that you're going to be

00:02:15.389 --> 00:02:16.709
responsible for later on.

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:18.360
The second thing to realize is that

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.940
a lot of the information that your school

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.907
is going to give you is bad.

00:02:21.907 --> 00:02:24.490
For example, I know that in UVA

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:30.276
the professors give over an
hour lecture on case briefing,

00:02:30.276 --> 00:02:33.908
how to highlight the
procedural posture of a case,

00:02:33.908 --> 00:02:37.442
how to separate out all the
different parts of a case.

00:02:37.442 --> 00:02:39.490
That information is completely useless.

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:41.898
Case briefs are just a waste of time

00:02:41.898 --> 00:02:44.996
and the information, it
comes from a good place,

00:02:44.996 --> 00:02:47.086
they want to give you some idea of what

00:02:47.086 --> 00:02:50.641
you should be doing, but it
really just fosters busy work.

00:02:50.641 --> 00:02:53.220
So don't feel bad if the
information that you're given

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.808
in orientation or the advice
that you got before law school

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:58.494
is contradictory or it
seems like it's creating

00:02:58.494 --> 00:03:01.109
more busy work than it
seems like the benefit

00:03:01.109 --> 00:03:02.308
that you're getting out of it.

00:03:02.308 --> 00:03:03.916
It's probably the case,
you're going to get

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:05.263
contradictory advice.

00:03:05.263 --> 00:03:07.922
And unless it's coming from
us it's probably bad advice.

00:03:07.922 --> 00:03:10.161
So don't feel like you
have to do everything

00:03:10.161 --> 00:03:12.228
that you were told before
going into law school

00:03:12.228 --> 00:03:14.306
and don't assume that just
because your law school

00:03:14.306 --> 00:03:17.400
tells you to do something
you should be doing it.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.049
Often it's the absolute
opposite of that case.

00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:21.484
With respect to case briefs,

00:03:21.484 --> 00:03:24.830
I see professors giving the
advice that all students

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:28.319
should case brief every
case that they get.

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:30.897
I think that's really terrible advice,

00:03:30.897 --> 00:03:32.624
in fact we have an entire video

00:03:32.624 --> 00:03:35.154
on why case briefing is bad
and why you shouldn't do it.

00:03:35.154 --> 00:03:37.701
If you feel you must case brief,

00:03:37.701 --> 00:03:40.308
do it once and then put it aside.

00:03:40.308 --> 00:03:43.004
I know the final exam might
seem like it's a long way off,

00:03:43.004 --> 00:03:45.501
but take a look at an issue spotting exam

00:03:45.501 --> 00:03:48.354
and see if there is any
way that you can imagine

00:03:48.354 --> 00:03:50.550
that you can use the
information of case brief

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.100
in the final exam.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.902
Odds are you can't, and I've
been doing this for a while,

00:03:54.902 --> 00:03:58.275
I've never seen a case where
using all of the information

00:03:58.275 --> 00:04:00.306
in a case brief actually
helped on the final exam,

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:03.656
I think it's just busy
work, it's a waste of time.

00:04:03.656 --> 00:04:05.850
Next, you're gonna meet a lot of people.

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.739
Think about forming a study group,

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:09.617
but don't put a lot of
pressure on yourself.

00:04:09.617 --> 00:04:12.205
Study groups can be great,
they're not always necessary,

00:04:12.205 --> 00:04:14.050
some people learn better in groups,

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.780
some people will feel like
they waste a lot of time

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.688
just going on in endless
conversation in study groups.

00:04:20.688 --> 00:04:23.784
Feel it out, perhaps
start up a study group,

00:04:23.784 --> 00:04:26.416
but don't feel bad if
you don't want to join

00:04:26.416 --> 00:04:28.587
study groups or if you
feel like the study groups

00:04:28.587 --> 00:04:29.620
are passing you by.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:30.894
Don't over commit.

00:04:30.894 --> 00:04:32.773
Now is the time to try things out.

00:04:32.773 --> 00:04:35.593
I know it feels like everything
is coming down on you

00:04:35.593 --> 00:04:37.243
and you're overwhelmed and slammed,

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:39.240
but really, you'll be able to separate

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.300
what's working for you and
what's not in the weeks to come.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.629
In that vein, don't try to do everything.

00:04:45.629 --> 00:04:48.621
Know that there's a limited
amount of time that you have

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:51.318
and you'll want to really
maximize the things that give you

00:04:51.318 --> 00:04:54.063
high return on investment,
things like outlining,

00:04:54.063 --> 00:04:56.916
things like practice exams,
things like distilling

00:04:56.916 --> 00:05:00.158
and synthesizing your notes
with commercial supplements.

00:05:00.158 --> 00:05:02.696
But don't try and do
absolutely everything.

00:05:02.696 --> 00:05:04.936
Don't try to join every extracurricular

00:05:04.936 --> 00:05:08.134
and don't join every single
intramural sports team.

00:05:08.134 --> 00:05:09.716
There is time for some of that stuff

00:05:09.716 --> 00:05:12.478
but don't feel like you
need to try everything out.

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:15.302
At the end of your first
week, what I would recommend

00:05:15.302 --> 00:05:17.177
is start outlining.

00:05:17.177 --> 00:05:20.430
Over the weekend what I liked
to do during my semester

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:24.002
was use your weekends for outlining.

00:05:24.002 --> 00:05:25.807
Distill all of your notes with all of your

00:05:25.807 --> 00:05:27.464
supplementary material.

00:05:27.464 --> 00:05:30.196
Your weekends are your time.

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:32.183
While during the week
you're going to class,

00:05:32.183 --> 00:05:35.133
you're preparing for class,
carve out your weekends

00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:36.705
to start outlining.

00:05:36.705 --> 00:05:38.533
And if you outline every single week

00:05:38.533 --> 00:05:40.700
by the time the study period
rolls around and finals,

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.840
you'll be studying from your outline

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:44.393
while everybody else is starting

00:05:44.393 --> 00:05:45.956
to put together their outline.

00:05:45.956 --> 00:05:49.466
Now, sure, there's value in
putting your outline together,

00:05:49.466 --> 00:05:52.502
but there's far more value in
getting that out of the way

00:05:52.502 --> 00:05:55.861
so you can study from it and
start using practice tests.

00:05:55.861 --> 00:05:58.068
And remember, even though it's week one

00:05:58.068 --> 00:05:59.733
you want to think about the endgame.

00:05:59.733 --> 00:06:01.388
It's all about finals.

00:06:01.388 --> 00:06:03.213
It's very rare that class participation

00:06:03.213 --> 00:06:04.672
counts for anything.

00:06:04.672 --> 00:06:07.895
So remember to always focus on the things

00:06:07.895 --> 00:06:10.573
that are going to provide
value at the final.

00:06:10.573 --> 00:06:13.603
It's never too early to
make that realization.

00:06:13.603 --> 00:06:16.381
And understand that it's
almost all about essays.

00:06:16.381 --> 00:06:19.746
Even in classes that have
some multiple choice questions

00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:21.970
the majority of your points
are gonna come from essays.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:24.238
And in most first year classes

00:06:24.238 --> 00:06:27.476
almost all of the points
come from issue spotting

00:06:27.476 --> 00:06:29.003
long form essays.

00:06:29.003 --> 00:06:31.750
And now is a good time to
think about the strategies

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.717
that go towards better essay writing

00:06:33.717 --> 00:06:35.694
because a lot of your points and a lot

00:06:35.694 --> 00:06:37.089
of your final grade is gonna be based

00:06:37.089 --> 00:06:38.392
on an issue spotting exam.

00:06:38.392 --> 00:06:41.873
Because so many professors
use issue spotting exams

00:06:41.873 --> 00:06:44.801
you can use the same
strategies in every class

00:06:44.801 --> 00:06:46.609
regardless of the substantive law.

00:06:46.609 --> 00:06:50.353
Legal Eagle offers an entire
class on mastering issue

00:06:50.353 --> 00:06:52.156
spotting exams, and
there are others as well

00:06:52.156 --> 00:06:53.811
that are also very good.

00:06:53.811 --> 00:06:56.942
But it's never too soon
to realize that most of

00:06:56.942 --> 00:07:00.062
your grade is going to come
from issue spotting exams.

00:07:00.062 --> 00:07:03.505
So the bottom line is, it's
week one, but don't freak out.

00:07:03.505 --> 00:07:05.454
You're gonna have the
entire rest of the semester

00:07:05.454 --> 00:07:06.860
to get into your groove.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.079
Everybody starts out in feeling like

00:07:09.079 --> 00:07:10.965
a stranger in a foreign land.

00:07:10.965 --> 00:07:13.551
Very little of what you're
learning in your first week

00:07:13.551 --> 00:07:15.490
is going to be on the test, so you can try

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:17.726
different methodologies
of preparing for class

00:07:17.726 --> 00:07:19.618
and working on your outline.

00:07:19.618 --> 00:07:21.405
You can find what works for you,

00:07:21.405 --> 00:07:23.548
and then really get
into your groove for the

00:07:23.548 --> 00:07:24.715
rest of the semester.

00:07:24.715 --> 00:07:27.141
I know it can seem
completely overwhelming,

00:07:27.141 --> 00:07:28.765
but that's okay, it happens to everyone.

00:07:28.765 --> 00:07:31.166
Now is a great time to take a deep breath,

00:07:31.166 --> 00:07:33.047
realize that it's all going to be okay,

00:07:33.047 --> 00:07:35.259
and think about the habits
that will get you across

00:07:35.259 --> 00:07:37.417
the goal line at the end of the semester.

00:07:37.417 --> 00:07:40.537
If you liked this video please
